<TopicPage slug="subjects/classics/foundational/homer-and-the-world-of-the-hero">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Classics, and it assumes nothing — no Greek, no mythology,
    no prior reading. You need only patience with unfamiliar names and a willingness to take
    a 2,700-year-old poem seriously on its own terms. Everything else is built here, step by
    step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what the Iliad and the Odyssey are, roughly when and how they were made, and why they are called the founding texts of Western literature;</li>
    <li>explain the heroic code — honour (<em>time</em>) and glory (<em>kleos</em>) — and use it to predict what a hero will do;</li>
    <li>recount the Iliad's plot as the anatomy of one man's rage, and the Odyssey's as a homecoming that tests whether a home can survive its hero;</li>
    <li>contrast the two heroes: Achilles' excellence is force, Odysseus' is <em>metis</em>, cunning intelligence;</li>
    <li>explain how oral composition works — formulae, type-scenes, the singer assembling verse live — and why this reframed the question "who was Homer?";</li>
    <li>trace the tradition's afterlife, from Virgil to modern film, and argue why Homer is still the template every later hero is measured against.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit governs the reading: the poems are performances as much as texts. Nearly every
    strange feature — the repetition, the lists, the speed — makes sense once you imagine a
    singer composing aloud for listeners who already know the story.
  </p>

  <h2>Two poems, and neither is what you expect</h2>
  <p>
    Classics in the West begins with a word for anger. The Iliad's first word is
    <em>menin</em> — rage, wrath — and it announces the poem's true subject: not the Trojan
    War, but one hero's fury inside it. The Odyssey, its companion, opens just as
    unexpectedly for an adventure story: its hero is stuck. Both poems were composed within a
    centuries-old singing tradition and written down at some point around the eighth or
    seventh century BCE, and both have been read continuously ever since — the first
    surviving, fully achieved studies of rage, grief, homecoming and fame.
  </p>
  <p>
    "The world of the hero" — the name this unit goes by — is doubly meant. It is a physical
    setting: Bronze Age palaces and camp-fires, recollected through the later dark age that
    followed them. And it is a moral universe with its own currency. <strong>Honour</strong>
    (<em>time</em>, pronounced "tee-may") is what heroes earn, exchange and die for;
    <strong>glory</strong> (<em>kleos</em>) is the fame that survives them. The two epics are
    the tradition's first great audit of that economy — the Iliad asking what the currency
    costs, the Odyssey asking what survives when it is gone.
  </p>
  <p>
    And neither poem is what a newcomer expects. The Iliad never mentions the wooden horse;
    it covers a few weeks in the war's tenth and final year, organised around a quarrel
    between two Greek commanders. The Odyssey is not a straight adventure but a homecoming
    told in flashes, where the monsters matter less than whether a marriage can survive
    twenty years of absence. The steps below map the whole territory before we walk it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two epics, one heroic universe" widget="equationStep" config={epicCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are one argument in sequence. The rage
    that opens the Iliad only makes sense inside the heroic code — which the Odyssey then
    revalues from the household's point of view. Step 4 explains why the poems look the way
    they do on the page, and step 5 is why they are still assigned: every later epic is read
    against these two. Keep the sequence in view as the sections below unpack each step.
  </p>

  <h2>The Iliad: rage and heroism</h2>
  <p>
    The plot is barely a war story: it is the anatomy of one man's anger and the price
    everyone else pays for it. Agamemnon, commander of the Greek forces, seizes Briseis,
    Achilles' war-prize and companion. Achilles, the greatest warrior alive, is dishonoured,
    withdraws from the fighting, and asks his divine mother Thetis to have Zeus turn the war
    against his own side until the Greeks learn his worth. The Greeks bleed. An embassy of
    old friends offers Achilles lavish compensation (Book 9, one of the poem's summit
    scenes); he refuses — what use is honour from people who took it? — until Patroclus, his
    dearest companion, wears Achilles' armour into battle and dies at Hector's hand. Rage
    returns Achilles to battle, he kills Hector and desecrates the body, and the poem ends
    not with victory but with the most famous scene of shared grief in Western literature:
    the old king Priam coming alone by night to kiss the hands of his son's killer, the two
    enemies weeping together — one for a father, one for a friend.
  </p>
  <p>
    The heroic code explains why the quarrel is unanswerable. A hero's worth is <em>time</em>
    — the visible respect paid in goods and words — and it behaves like a finite currency:
    praise given to another is praise not given to you. Achilles had been given a choice of
    two fates: a long life at home with no glory, or a short life at Troy with <em>kleos</em>
    forever. He came to Troy knowing this. Homer invented, in the very first texts we have,
    the idea that a war story could be a tragedy of the victors — and the poem keeps its
    power because its battlefield is rendered without glory's varnish: the spear that catches
    a man beside the eye, the dogs, the dust, the widows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two scenes do the poem's deepest work. Book 6 gives Hector, the Trojan champion and the
    poem's most human hero, his farewell on the city wall: his wife Andromache begs him to
    stay, his baby son is frightened by the horsehair plume on his helmet, and Hector laughs,
    removes it, and takes the boy up — before walking back to a battle he knows he will lose,
    because honour leaves him no choice the poem could respect. And the gods, who can seem
    like fairy-tale machinery, function as a sharp commentary on the code: Zeus watches
    mortals die for an honour the gods manipulate for amusement, and the poem lets the
    mismatch stand. The Iliad is simultaneously the greatest advertisement for the heroic
    ideal and its most corrosive critique — which is exactly why the tradition never stopped
    arguing with it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Achilles was invulnerable except for his heel. That
  detail is a much later invention — the Roman-era poet Statius supplied the Styx-dipping —
  and in Homer, Achilles is fully mortal, which is precisely what gives his choice its
  weight. Between a long life without glory and a short one with it, nothing is pre-decided
  by magic anatomy; when he falls (in poems of the cycle after the Iliad), it is to an arrow
  in battle. Subtract the heel and the heroic code comes back into focus: <em>kleos</em>
  bought with a real death.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Iliad tells the Trojan War, ending with the wooden
  horse. The horse belongs to other traditions (and appears in the Odyssey as a story told
  at a feast); the Iliad ends with Hector's funeral — the war unfinished, as everyone in the
  audience knew it must be. The poem's selectivity is its art: by choosing a few weeks of a
  ten-year war and one hero's anger as its subject, it makes the war a lens for mortality
  rather than a sequence of events.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the Iliad runs the heroic code at full power and records the
    cost — honour won, spent, and mourned. The Odyssey takes the same world and asks its
    question from the other end.
  </p>

  <h2>The Odyssey: homecoming and cunning</h2>
  <p>
    The Odyssey opens where war epics close: with survival as the problem. Odysseus is
    stranded a decade after Troy, held on an island by the nymph Calypso, who offers him
    immortality — the heroic currency revalued as a trap — while back home his house fills
    with suitors consuming his estate and pressing his wife Penelope to remarry. The poem's
    structure sets its values: the first four books follow the son, Telemachus, learning to
    act; the central books let Odysseus narrate his own wanderings (the Cyclops Polyphemus,
    blinded by the trick of the name "Nobody"; Circe; the Sirens, survived by being lashed to
    the mast; the land of the dead); the last twelve deliver the return in disguise, the bow
    contest, and the slaughter and reunion the whole poem has been aimed at.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where Achilles' excellence is force, Odysseus' is <em>metis</em> — cunning intelligence,
    the trick that wins when strength cannot: endure the Cyclops' cave until escape is
    possible, lie his way through his own hall, weep convincingly at his own story told by
    others. And the poem's moral centre is domestic. The Odyssey audits the heroic world from
    the household's point of view: fidelity is tested not on battlefields but in bedrooms and
    weaving rooms. Penelope, unravelling her weaving nightly for three years to stall the
    suitors, is the poem's quiet hero, and the final test of identity is the marriage bed —
    rooted to a living olive tree, a secret only the true husband could know. Hospitality
    (<em>xenia</em>) is the poem's working ethic: every door tells you whether a society is
    civilised by how it treats the stranger at the threshold, and the Cyclops — who eats his
    guests — is the counter-case that defines the value.
  </p>
  <p>
    The return is deliberately double. Odysseus regains his house in a night of slaughter,
    and the modern reader gets a second, quieter test in the aftermath — the hanging of the
    disloyal maidservants, ordered without ceremony, sits in the poem without comment and
    refuses to sit in the reader without discomfort. That friction is productive, not a flaw:
    the Odyssey holds up the heroic world's own mirror and lets a contemporary audience see
    both the grandeur and the cruelty the code priced in. The hero is home; what the return
    cost, the poem does not let you uncount.
  </p>

  <h2>Epic technique and the oral tradition</h2>
  <p>
    The epics' strangest surface feature — the repetition — is the key to how they were
    made. "Swift-footed Achilles" appears even when he is sitting down; "rosy-fingered Dawn"
    and "the wine-dark sea" return book after book; arming scenes, feasts and sacrifices
    unfold in standard blocks. These are not tics but <em>formulae</em>: ready-made phrases
    metrically shaped to fill a line of <strong>dactylic hexameter</strong> (the epic's
    six-beat line), letting a singer compose fluent, rapid verse in real time — the way an
    improvising musician reaches for memorised licks. Around the formulae hang
    <em>type-scenes</em> — the arming, the sacrifice, the arrival — assembled and varied like
    musical phrases.
  </p>
  <p>
    This observation was the twentieth century's great Homerological discovery. Milman
    Parry, in the 1920s and 1930s, showed that the formula system is too systematic to be
    literary habit and too necessary to be decoration — and tested the point by recording
    living oral poets in Yugoslavia, who composed epic in performance using exactly such
    traditional phraseology. His collaborator Albert Lord published the synthesis as <em>The
    Singer of Tales</em> (1960). The "Homeric question" — were the poems one genius's work
    or a composite? — was reframed: the Iliad and Odyssey are the crystallised peak of a
    centuries-long singing tradition, unified by an extraordinary poet or poets, and fixed by
    writing at some point in the eighth to sixth centuries BCE (an ancient tradition credits
    Athens under the tyrant Pisistratus; scholars still argue). One performer within the
    Odyssey — the blind bard Demodocus, singing at a feast — is Homer's own picture of how
    these poems lived before they were books; the Iliad runs to 15,693 lines and the Odyssey
    to 12,110, each in 24 books, and every statistical oddity of the text is evidence about
    that oral life.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "Homer wrote the Iliad and the Odyssey" the way a
  novelist writes a novel — alone, with a pen, once. The oral-formulaic evidence reframed
  the question: the poems are the crystallised peak of a centuries-old singing tradition,
  assembled in performance from inherited formulae and type-scenes, and fixed by writing at
  some point in the eighth to sixth centuries BCE. Whether that fixing was the work of one
  extraordinary poet, two, or a tradition given a name, the text itself will not say — which
  is why the honest answer to "who was Homer?" is an account of how oral poetry works.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two poems, one heroic universe — the Iliad pricing the code, the
    Odyssey auditing it from home, and both built as live performance from inherited
    phraseology. Now the code itself, and its long shadow.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What are <em>time</em> and <em>kleos</em>, and how does each behave?</li>
    <li>Why does the Iliad end with Hector's funeral rather than the fall of Troy?</li>
    <li>Give one scene where Odysseus wins by <em>metis</em> rather than force, and say what the trick is.</li>
    <li>What did Parry and Lord's fieldwork show about how the epics were composed?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) <em>Time</em> is honour — the visible respect paid in
    goods and words — and it works like a finite currency: praise given to another is praise
    not given to you. <em>Kleos</em> is the glory that survives death; it is why Achilles
    chose a short life at Troy. (2) The poem's subject is rage and its cost, not the war's
    outcome; ending on Priam and Achilles weeping together makes the war a lens for mortality.
    (3) The Cyclops: he tells the monster his name is "Nobody", so when the blinded Polyphemus
    screams that Nobody is hurting him, no neighbours come. (4) That South Slavic epic singers
    compose long poems in performance from traditional formulae and type-scenes — showing the
    Homeric repetitions are the machinery of oral composition, not decoration.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The Iliad and Odyssey — written down around the eighth or seventh century BCE from an oral singing tradition — are the founding texts of Western literature.</li>
    <li>The heroic code: honour (<em>time</em>) is the hero's finite currency; glory (<em>kleos</em>) is fame that outlives the hero; Achilles chose a short glorious life.</li>
    <li>The Iliad is organised by rage (<em>menis</em>), covers a few weeks of the war's tenth year, and ends with grief, not victory — a tragedy of the victors.</li>
    <li>The Odyssey flips the values: <em>metis</em> beats force, <em>xenia</em> defines civilisation, and the real tests are domestic — Penelope and the marriage bed.</li>
    <li>Formulae and type-scenes are the machinery of live oral composition; Parry and Lord proved it from living singers.</li>
    <li>"Who was Homer?" is best answered by describing how oral poetry works — a tradition crystallised, possibly by one extraordinary poet, into two texts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lessons in this subject read the tradition Homer started: Virgil's Aeneid
    answering both epics for Augustan Rome, Greek tragedy reworking the same heroic material
    on stage, and the art and archaeology that picture the world the poems remember. Each
    later work takes the heroic code you have just met and bends it to its own century —
    which is the surest sign the code is the syllabus's real subject.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Oral composition changes how you read everything. When Achilles' shield is described for
    hundreds of lines, that is not padding but the tradition doing what it does brilliantly —
    <em>ekphrasis</em>, the virtuoso set-piece — and when a character's fate is foreshadowed
    repeatedly, that is how a tradition that expects its audience to know the story sustains
    tension anyway: you listen to hear <em>how</em>, not what. Ring composition (a passage
    returning to its starting point before moving on), extended similes that slide from
    battlefield to ordinary life, and scenes of ordinary people — the swineherd, the slave
    women, the old dog Argos who recognises his master and dies — are technique and moral
    vision at once. And the poems remember an older world imperfectly on purpose: Bronze Age
    palaces and boar's-tusk helmets surface in poems where heroes are cremated (a later
    practice), a mixture archaeology confirms as an honest dark-age memory of a vanished
    world.
  </p>
  <p>
    The afterlife is the other half of the subject. Virgil built the Aeneid on the two poems'
    architecture (war epic and wandering epic); Dante made Virgil his guide; Joyce's Ulysses
    mapped a Dublin day onto the Odyssey. In the twentieth century the scholar Joseph
    Campbell distilled a "monomyth" — the hero's journey, a pattern he traced through world
    mythology: the hero ventures from the common day into a region of supernatural wonder,
    wins a decisive victory, and returns with a boon for his people. Film-makers from George
    Lucas onward have used the pattern consciously — and the liveliest recent films
    deliberately tease it, filling their casts with rogues who keep missing their cue for the
    pattern's low point, as if to ask how tired the template has become. Homer is the
    pattern's fountainhead, and two millennia of translation debates (Lattimore's fidelity,
    Fagles' speed, Emily Wilson's bid to make the Odyssey new) are part of the tradition too:
    each generation re-sings the songs — which is, if Parry and Lord are right, exactly what
    the poems have always been for.
  </p>

